---
title: Sequence Removal
sidebar:
  order: 17
---

Remove sequences by sequence ID. It is the opposite of [sequence extraction](/docs/api-usage/python/sequence_extract/), and is faster when you remove fewer than half of the sequences.

## Example

Set the input with `input_dir` or `input_files`, then call a removal method.

```python
import pysegul

def remove_sequences():
    remove = pysegul.SequenceRemoval(
        input_fmt='nexus',
        datatype='dna',
        output_path='tests/output',
        output_format='fasta',
        )
    remove.input_dir = 'tests/align-data'
    remove.remove_id_list(['abce1', 'abce2'])
```

## Options

| Option | Default | Description |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `input_fmt` | Required | `'auto'`, `'fasta'`, `'nexus'`, `'phylip'` |
| `datatype` | Required | `'dna'`, `'aa'`, `'ignore'` |
| `output_path` | Required | Output directory |
| `output_format` | Required | `'fasta'`, `'nexus'`, `'phylip'`, or interleaved `'fasta-int'`, `'nexus-int'`, `'phylip-int'` |

## Input

| Attribute | Description |
| --- | --- |
| `input_dir` | Directory of input sequence files |
| `input_files` | List of input sequence files |

## Methods

| Method | Remove sequences |
| --- | --- |
| `remove_regex(regex)` | whose IDs match a regular expression ([Rust regex syntax](https://docs.rs/regex/latest/regex/)) |
| `remove_id_list(id)` | with the IDs in a list |
